window.SAQ_PAPER_QUESTIONS["2020-2-northern-sydney"]=[{"number":1,"title":"","stemText":"48 year old female presents with palpitations and light-headedness. An ECG is obtained An image is available in the Props Book","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Describe and interpret the ECG (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List 4 causes of an irregular, wide complex tachycardia (4 marks)\nDiscuss two options for treatment of this dysrhythmia (6 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:imge4eaddbb5e8dccc201"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Irregularly irregular</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Wide complex QRS</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Appearances suggest AF with aberrancy, pre-existing  bundle, or conduction down a bypass</div><div class=\"answer-line\">tract of  WPW</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Uncommon but important to recognize. Two features that suggest a bypass  tract - the</div><div class=\"answer-line\">extremely rapid rate with R-R intervals  at a rate of 300bpm and the varying morphology of</div><div class=\"answer-line\">the QRS complexes</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Intermittent narrow cx QRS from occasional normal AV conduction</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2020.2</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false,"investigationTables":[{"id":"investigation-1","title":"Venous blood gas","headers":["Test","Result"],"rows":[["pH","7.50"],["pCO2","44"],["HCO3","37"],["BE","12.1"],["Na","138"],["K","3.1"],["Cl","90"],["BSL","8.9"],["Lactate","1.6"],["Hb","156"]]}]},{"number":2,"title":"","stemText":"A 78 year-old male is brought to the emergency department after a fall down 6 steps. The fall was unwitnessed. On assessment he is haemodynamically stable, amnesic to the event of the fall. He has atrial fibrillation and is on regular dabigatran.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"On examination he has midline cervical tenderness. Describe the significant findings on the CT Cervical Spine Image (3 marks) An image is available in the Props Book"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Describe the clinical findings expected with central cord syndrome (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Explain the neuroanatomical distribution of the spinal tracts which causes this clinical pattern (1 mark)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"The acute spinal surgeon requests that the anticoagulant should be reversed. Describe the mechanism of action of dabigatran and the reversal agent Idarucizumab (Praxabind) (4 marks)"},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"CT Trauma has revealed the acute unstable cervical spine injury only. The patient is placed in a semi- rigid collar and spinal immobilisation protocols followed. After several hours in the department he develops significant stridor and emergent intubation is planned. List the factors that will affect the percentage of glottic opening seen at laryngoscopy specific to this patient (3 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:img7291827b6fd3629c4b"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Multilevel degenerative changes throughout the cervical spine</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Disc-osteophyte complexes multi-level</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Canal stenosis is most significant (moderate-severe) at C5-6</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•      Cervical pre-vertebral haematoma C1-C6</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Upper limbs neuro deficit &gt;Lower limbs</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2020.2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Motor neuro deficit &gt; Sensory deficit</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Distal neuro deficit &gt; proximal deficit</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Spinal tracts of the upper limb lie more central in the corticospinal and spinothalamic tracts.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Idaracizumab: Monoclonal antibody. <em>Binds:</em> unbound dabigatran, thrombin-bound</div><div class=\"answer-line\">dabigatran active glucuronide metabolites of dabigatran. Once dabigatran is complexed</div><div class=\"answer-line\">to <em>idarucizumab</em>, the anticoagulant effects are neutralized.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>CT Trauma has revealed the acute unstable cervical spine injury only. The patient is placed in a </strong></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>List the factors that will affect the percentage of glottic opening seen at laryngoscopy specific to </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>this patient? (3 marks) </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Indication for ongoing MILS of C-spine</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Supraglottic swelling posteriorly from pre-vertebral cervical haematoma</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pre-existing cervical spondylosis/kyphosis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2020.2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2020.2</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":3,"title":"","stemText":"62 year old female presents to your Emergency Department with 2 months of persistent cough and a new onset of haemoptysis. She estimates that she has coughed up half a cup of\nblood in the last 24 hours.\nShe has no significant medical history and takes no medications.\n\nVital signs:\n\nHR 105/min\n\nBP 95/50\n\nSats 92% on room air\n\nRR 18/min\nA CXR is performed. An image is available in the Props Book","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Describe the CXR (3 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List your differential diagnoses (3 marks) Outline the key steps in the initial treatment of life-threatening haemoptysis (7 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:img7d26d91d4b7151825f"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">62 year old female presents to your Emergency Department with 2 months of persistent</div><div class=\"answer-line\">cough and a new onset of haemoptysis. She estimates that she has coughed up half a cup of</div><div class=\"answer-line\">blood in the last 24 hours.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">She has no significant medical history and takes no medications.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">BP 95/50</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Sats 92% on room air</div><div class=\"answer-line\">RR 18/min</div><div class=\"answer-line\">A CXR is performed.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">An image is available in the Props Book</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":4,"title":"","stemText":"A 47-year-old man has sustained a high-voltage electrical injury in an industrial accident. He has been intubated pre-hospital.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List the factors that determine the severity of electrical injuries (5 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List the potential causes of difficulty with mechanical ventilation specific to a patient with a high- voltage electrical injury (4 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"The patient is found to have dark coloured urine and a creatinine kinase (CK) elevated at 41,000 U/L. What are the potential pathophysiological causes of rhabdomyolysis in electrical injuries? (3 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Outline your management of the rhabdomyolysis (4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Type of current (AC generally worse than DC). DC current tends to cause single muscle</div><div class=\"answer-line\">spasm and throws victim, therefore shorter duration of contact but higher likelihood of</div><div class=\"answer-line\">trauma</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Duration of exposure</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Resistance of tissues (area of contact, pressure applied and presence of moisture) – higher</div><div class=\"answer-line\">resistance (skin, bone and fat) → greater thermal injury. Dry skin has a resistance of about</div><div class=\"answer-line\">100,000ohms, this drops to less than 2500ohms when skin is dampened</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Voltage “high” generally &gt; 1000V, or if AC - cycles per second or Hz – skeletal muscle can</div><div class=\"answer-line\">become titanic with frequencies between 15-150Hz</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Current (amps) – 10-12mA causes pain, 100mA causes VF. Amount of AC needed to cause</div><div class=\"answer-line\">injury varies in proportion to its frequency (ie. Lower current required at lower frequency)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pathway of current</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Chest compartment syndrome due to circumferential or extensive thoracic burns</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pulmonary oedema</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Lung contusion from associated trauma</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Tetany and insufficient paralysis / ventilator dyssynchrony</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Direct muscle necrosis from electrothermal injury</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Muscle injury from compartment syndrome associated with muscle oedema and/or</div><div class=\"answer-line\">cutaneous scarring</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ischaemic muscle injury subsequent to vascular thrombosis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Global hypoxic insult due to cardiac arrest</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Prevent further rhabdo – assess for compartment syndrome, consider fasciotomies etc</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">•</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Correct volume depletion – fluid replacement to establish adequate UO. Parkland formula</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">not to be used following electrical injuries since surface burns grossly underestimates extent</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">of injury</td></tr><tr><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td><td>FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</td><td>2020.2</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">•</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Minimise AKI – alkaline diuresis – IV bicarb (ensure hypocalcaemia not present, arterial pH is</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">less than 7.5, serum bicarb less than 30), rate adjusted to achieve urine ph of &gt;6.5</td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td>Manage established renal injury → CRRT</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">•</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Mannitol not thought to be useful these days</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">•</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Loop diuretics have no impact on outcome of AKI, may worsen hypocalcaemia as they</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">induce calcium is and may increase cast formation (MAY be used if volume overload present)</td></tr><tr><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td><td>FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</td><td>2020.2</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":5,"title":"","stemText":"Whilst the incidence of contrast induced nephropathy and contrast induced reaction is low, it is considered sensible practice to screen patients on the basis of their past history.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Before the administration of iodinated contrast, what past history should be elucidated from the patient to assess risk that may be associated with contrast administration. Specify why for each aspect of the history? (12 marks) History or condition Explanation as to why important"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Name 4 drugs from 4 different classes that should be withheld or dose-adjusted if the patient is at risk of renal impairment post contrast (4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Hyperthyroidism, thyroid nodule or goitre Exacerbate due to iodine load</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Contrast allergy Increases risk of repeat reaction 60x</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Renal impairment, solitary kidney, renal cancer, Increased risk of contrast induce</td></tr><tr><td>renal surgery</td><td>nephrotoxicity particularly eGFR</td></tr><tr><td>&lt;30ml/min; probably higher risk &lt;</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>45ml/min</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>History of diabetes, particularly metformin use Diabetes increase risk of CIN, metformin</td></tr><tr><td>renally excreted and can cause lactic</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>acidosis if renal impairment ensues</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Multiple allergies 3-5x risk of reaction</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>asthma Increased risk of worsening allergy if</td></tr><tr><td>occurs (6x and high risk with unstable</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>disease)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Myasthenia gravis</td><td>Increased risk of worsening weakness</td></tr><tr><td>B Blocker use</td><td>Increased risk of moderate to severe</td></tr><tr><td>reaction if allergy occurs</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Sickle cell anaemia</td><td>Increased risk of pain episode</td></tr><tr><td>Cardiac failure</td><td>Increased risk of Contrast induced</td></tr><tr><td>nephropathy</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Advanced age</td><td>Increased risk of CIN</td></tr><tr><td>Hypertension</td><td>Increased risk CIN</td></tr><tr><td>Concomitant nephrotoxin use</td><td>Increased risk CIN</td></tr><tr><td>Periprocedural hypotension</td><td>Increased risk CIN</td></tr><tr><td>myeloma</td><td>Increased risk of CIN if hypercalcaemic</td></tr><tr><td>+/- dehydrated</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Interleukin 2 therapy in last 6 months</td><td>Increased risk of allergy</td></tr><tr><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td><td>FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers 2020.2</td></tr><tr><td>Essential to cite contrast allergy specifically <strong>and</strong> renal impairment to pass</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"3\">risk of renal impairment post iv contrast.</td></tr><tr><td><em><strong> </strong></em><strong>Anti-infectives </strong></td><td>Aciclovir, famciclovir, ganciclovir, valaciclovir,</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Antiviral - Guanine analogues</td><td>valganciclovir</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Antiviral - Azoles</td><td>Fluconazole</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Aminoglycosides</td><td>Amikacin, gentamicin, tobramycin, vancomycin</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Carbepenem</td><td>Ertapenem, imipenem, meropenem</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Cephalosporins</td><td>Cefazolin, cefepime, cefotaxime, ceftazidime,</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Quinolones</td><td>cefuroxime</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Penicillins</td><td>Ciprofloxacin, norfloxacin</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Sulfonamides</td><td>Piperacillin</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Other</td><td>Sulfamethoxazole</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Nitrofurantoin, trimethoprim</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Angiotensin Converting Enzyme Inhibitors </strong></td><td>Captopril, enalapril, fosinopril, lisinopril, perindopril,</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>(ACE inhibitors)</td><td>quinapril, ramipril, trandolapril</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">(See also NB3)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Angiotensin 2 Receptor Antagonists </strong></td><td>Candesartan, eprosartan, irbesartan, losartan,</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>(ARBs or ARAs)</td><td>olmesartan, telmisartan, valsartan</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">(See also NB3)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Anticoagulants </strong></td><td>Dabigatran</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Direct thrombin inhibitors</td><td>Apixaban, rivaroxaban</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Factor Xa inhibitors</td><td>Enoxaparin</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Heparins</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Antidepressants </strong></td><td>Desvenlafaxine, duloxetine, venlafaxine</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Antidiabetic agents </strong></td><td>Insulin</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Sulfonylureas</td><td>Metformin (See also NB1)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>SLGT2 inhibitors</td><td>Glibenclamide, gliclazide, glimepiride, glipizide</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Dapagliflozin, empagliflozin, ertugliflozin (See also</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">NB2)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Benzodiazepines </strong></td><td>Alprazolam, bromazepam, clobazam, clonazepam,</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">diazepam, flunitrazepam, lorazepam, midazolam,</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">nitrazepam, oxazepam, temazepam</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NSAIDs </strong></td><td>Celecoxib, Diclofenac, Ibuprofen, Indometacin,</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Ketoprofen, Ketorolac, Mefenamic acid, Meloxicam,</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Naproxen, Parecoxib, Piroxicam</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Opioids </strong></td><td>Codeine, hydromorphone, morphine, oxycodone,</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">tramadol</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Other/Miscellaneous </strong></td><td>Allopurinol</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Atenolol</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Azathioprine</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Baclofen</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Colchicine</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Digoxin</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Eplerenone</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Fenofibrate</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Gabapentin and Pregabalin</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Levetiracetam</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Lithium</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Methenamine Hippurate</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Methotrexate</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Pramipexole</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Sotalol</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Spironolactone</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Topiramate</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Triamterene</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Zoledronic acid</td></tr><tr><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td><td>FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</td><td>2020.2</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":6,"title":"","stemText":"A 3 year old well girl presents to your Urban District Emergency Department. Mum describes a loss\n• f consciousness whilst running at Junior Soccer Superstars and mentions that her cousin died as a\nchild from an unknown cause.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 6 causes of childhood syncope (6 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"You perform a detailed history and examination. Vitals are within normal range and BSL is 4.8mmol/L. You ask the RN to perform an ECG. An image is available in the Props Book.\nGive 4 findings and your interpretation of these findings on this ECG (5 marks)\nWhat are your 3 priorities in the ongoing assessment and management of this child? (3 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:img1377f66da7bc483e01"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">1.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">5.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">6.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Answers</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Vasovagal syncope (64-73%)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cardiac 3-5%- Primary electrical disturbance-long or short QT/WPW/VT/Brugada/AV</div><div class=\"answer-line\">block/Sinus node dysfunction</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Structural Heart disease-Cardiomyopathies, Arythmogenic right ventricular dysplasia,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pulmonary hypertension, congenital heart disease, AS or other valve lesions</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Breath holding spell/reflex anoxic seizures (6.4%)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Neurologic-seizures/basilar migraine/ vertiginous drop attack</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Metabolic- dehydration/hypoglycaemia/orthostatic hypotension</div><div class=\"answer-line\">You perform a detailed history and examination.  Vitals are within normal range and BSL is</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4.8mmol/L.  You ask the RN to perform an ECG.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Heart rate of 110 bpm (normal for age).</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Dominant R waves in V1-3.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2020.2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Partial RBBB (RSR’ pattern in V1).</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Juvenile T-wave pattern (T wave inversion in V1-3).</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":7,"title":"","stemText":"An 86-year-old woman is brought to your ED by relatives concerned that she has become confused\n• ver the last few days. The patient says that she has felt a bit unwell and off her food but has no\nspecific complaints. She is usually cognitively normal and lives independently. She has a background\n• f hypertension, GORD, mild COPD and mild cardiomegaly and congestive cardiac failure.\nHer normal medications are -\nAtorvastatin 20mg morning\n-\nPropranolol 10mg BD\n-\nEzetimibe 10mg morning\n-\nFluticazone/vilanterol 200/25 one puff daily\n-\nPantoprazole 20mg night\n-\nVerapamil SR 240mg morning\nOn examination she looks a little dehydrated and is mildly confused. She has the following vital signs:\n\nHR 26 bpm\n\nBP 105/55\n\nTemp 37 C\n\nGCS 15\n\nSats 97% on air\nAn ECG is obtained An image is available in the Props Book","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Describe the ECG (3 marks) Initial venous blood gas results show:\n\npH\n7.28 (7.35-7.45)\n\npO2\n51 mmHg (-)\n\npCO2\n19 mmHg (41-51) Bicarb 16 mmol/L (22-26)\n\nBE\n-8.0 (-2 to +2) Sodium 136 mmol/L (135-145) Potassium 6.7 mmol/L (3.5-5.5)\n\nCreatinine\n200 umol/L (62-106)\n\nGlucose\n5.2 mmol/L (2-8)\n\nLactate\n3.7 mmol/L (0.5-2)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Provide a brief explanation of the likely pathophysiological process causing this clinical picture (4 marks) Outline four of the important clinical issues in this presentation and the concurrent treatments that should be undertaken (8 marks) Issue Treatment(s)"}],"propPages":["asset:imgc30e25e59c915fd380"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">An 86-year-old woman is brought to your ED by relatives concerned that she has become confused</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">over the last few days. The patient says that she has felt a bit unwell and off her food but has no</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">specific complaints. She is usually cognitively normal and lives independently. She has a background</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">of hypertension, GORD, mild COPD and mild cardiomegaly and congestive cardiac failure.</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Her normal medications are</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Atorvastatin 20mg morning</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Propranolol 10mg BD</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Ezetimibe 10mg morning</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Verapamil SR 240mg morning</div><div class=\"answer-line\">On examination she looks a little dehydrated and is mildly confused. She has the following vital</div><div class=\"answer-line\">signs:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">HR 26 bpm</div><div class=\"answer-line\">BP 105/55</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Temp 37 C</div><div class=\"answer-line\">GCS 15</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Sats 97% on air</div><div class=\"answer-line\">An ECG is obtained</div><div class=\"answer-line\">An image is available in the Props Book</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2020.2</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":8,"title":"","stemText":"A 56 y.o. male represents to your department with vomiting. He was seen in ED last night by a JMO and sent home with a diagnosis of gastroenteritis. He has been able to tolerate small amounts of fluids, but his vomiting is ongoing. He has a background of hypertension.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 4 causes of vomiting in the absence of abdominal pain (4 marks)\nHis venous blood gas is as follows:\n\npH\n7.50\n\npCO2\n\nHCO3\n\nBE\n12.1\n\nNa\n138\n\nK\n3.1\n\nCl\n\nBSL\n8.9\n\nLactate\n1.6\n\nHb\n156"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"What are the 2 main findings on this gas (2 marks)\nComplete the following table in relation to the above acid base disturbance. (8 marks) Cause of acid base disturbance Example You suspect some form of gastric outlet obstruction."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"What are some of the underlying pathologies that can cause this problem? (4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">A 56 y.o. male represents to your department with vomiting. He was seen in ED last night by</div><div class=\"answer-line\">a JMO and sent home with a diagnosis of gastroenteritis. He has been able to tolerate small</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Distinguishing feature</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>amounts of fluids, but his vomiting is ongoing.</td><td>He has a background of hypertension.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"empty-state\">The supplied marking resource did not contain extractable text for this part.</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":false,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":9,"title":"","stemText":"A 35-year-old female with a history of chronic marijuana use presents with intractable vomiting for\nthe past 36 hours.\nShe appears dehydrated. Her abdomen is mildly distended with no significant tenderness. She is moderately drowsy and has ongoing vomiting and abdominal pain in the ED. She has no other background history Her blood results are as below:\n\nNa 110 mmol/L (135-145)\n\nK 4.2 mmol/L (3.5-5.5)\n\nCl 75 mmol/L (97-107)\nBicarb 29 mmol/L (22-26)\n\nUrea 42.5 mmol/L (2.5-7.1)\n\nCreatinine 320 umol/L (62-106)\n\nWCC 17.5 x 10^9 (4-10)\n\nHb 160 g/L (115-160)\n\nPlatelets 393 x 10^9 (150-400)\n\nBSL 5.6 mmol/L (3.9-7.1)\nBeta HCG negative","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Her blood tests are shown. Describe and interpret the blood test findings (3 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Assuming the aetiology of her vomiting is marijuana hyperemesis syndrome, outline some treatment\n• ptions available (4 marks)\nComplete the table indicating the features of acute moderate hyponatraemia versus acute severe hyponatraemia (4 marks) Degree of Severity Moderate Severe Clinical features Serum sodium (mmol/L)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Briefly describe your immediate treatment of this patient’s acute hyponatraemia (4 marks) Book Two"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Severe hyponatraemia – severe in terms of actual number and because of the presence of</div><div class=\"answer-line\">symptoms</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">AKI, presumably on the basis of vomiting → pre-renal.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Elevated WCC – could relate to stress response/vomiting – could indicate intra-abdominal or</div><div class=\"answer-line\">other sepsis.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hypochloraemic Alkalosis – likely metabolic with the history of vomiting</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Assuming the aetiology of her vomiting is marijuana hyperemesis syndrome, outline some</div><div class=\"answer-line\">treatment options available (4 marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Supportive – e.g. IV fluids, PPI</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Anti-emetics – metoclopramide, ondansetron – frequently ineffective</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Haloperidol and/or benzodiazepines e.g. midazolam, lorazapam -  may be effective</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hot showers</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Some evidence for topical capsaicin cream</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Recommend cannabis cessation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2020.2</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Complete the table indicating the features of acute moderate hyponatraemia versus acute</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">severe hyponatraemia (4 marks)</td></tr><tr><td>Degree of Severity</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Severe</td></tr><tr><td>Clinical features</td><td>Nausea without vomiting</td><td>Vomiting</td></tr><tr><td>Confusion</td><td>Coma</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Headache</td><td>Seizure</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Non specific – headache,</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">fatigue, lethargy, dizziness, gait</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">disturbance, confusion, muscle</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">cramps</td></tr><tr><td>Serum sodium (mmol/L)</td><td>120-129 mmol/L</td><td>&lt; 120 mmol/L</td></tr><tr><td>iv.</td><td>Briefly describe your immediate treatment of this patient’s acute hyponatraemia (3 marks)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">•</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">100mL (or 2-3 mL/kg) 3% HTS over 10 - 20 min</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">•</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Reassess patient, if symptoms persist this can be repeated up to 2 additional 100mL doses</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">•</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">OR – x2 150mL blouses of 3% over 20 minutes, measuring serum sodium in between</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">infusions</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">•</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">The goal is to rapidly increase the serum sodium by 4-6 mmol/L over a period of a few hours,</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">raising it by 4 – 6 should alleviate symptoms and prevent hernia Timon</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">•</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Do not use mannitol in these patients (potentially nephrotic is and can lower Na).</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Vasopressin antagonists have variable efficacy and action too slow</td></tr><tr><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td><td>FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</td><td>2020.2</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":10,"title":"","stemText":"A 73 year old man presents following a fall from standing height while playing tennis. He complains\n• f pain in his right shoulder and pleuritic right-sided chest pain.\nHe has a past medical history of antithrombin III deficiency and previous DVTs, for which he is on lifelong warfarin therapy. His examination findings are as follows:\n\nRR 18\n\nSats 98% r/a\n\nHR 87\n\nBP 145/85\nMild diffuse tenderness of the right anterior chest wall Tense swelling overlying the right deltoid","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"A CXR is obtained. Please list 3 CXR findings (3 marks) An image is displayed in the Props Book What findings on history and clinical examination would justify CT imaging in order to identify traumatic thoracic injuries? (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"While in the emergency department, the patient suddenly develops severe respiratory distress. On examination:\n\nRR 40\n\nSats 80% on air\n\nHR 110\n\nBP 70/40\nWhat are your immediate management priorities? (4 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"The patient stabilizes and is transferred to the intensive care unit for ongoing observation. He is alert and spontaneously ventilating on 2L O2 via nasal prongs. List 3 possible pharmacological pain management strategies for this condition, and an advantage and disadvantage of each (6 marks) Advantage Disadvantage Option"}],"propPages":["asset:img045d6d2308521f6b45"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Fractures right ribs 8-10</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Large R sided pneumothorax</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Right sided pleural effusion – presumably haemothorax</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Possibly increased lung markings on left suggesting shift</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Medastinal contour normal, no subcut air</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2020.2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">High flow O2 via NRBM – aim normal sats &gt;95% / preoxygenation for potential intubation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Decompress L PTX – 18G IVC 2nd IC space mid-clavicular line</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Insert intercostal catheter L chest</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Prothrombinex-VF 30-50U/kg aim INR &lt;1.5 or FFP 2 units aim INR &lt;1.5</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2020.2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Vitamin K 3mg IV – in combination with above to prolong duration of anticoagulation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">reversal you could probably choose any dose up to 10mg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Fluid bolus or other sensible suggestion</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Advantage</th><th>Disadvantage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Paracetamol 1g TDS po</td><td>Few SEs, suitable for outpatient</td><td>May be inadequate in acute</td></tr><tr><td>use, no requirement for IVC</td><td>stage</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>NSAIDs eg Ibuprofen 400mg tds</td><td>Good analgesia, synergistic with</td><td>GI effects – bleeding, PLT effects,</td></tr><tr><td>po</td><td>opioids, suitable for outpatient</td><td>renal impairment</td></tr><tr><td>use</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Oral opioid analgesia eg</td><td>Suitable for outpatient use, no</td><td>Possible tolerance, dependence,</td></tr><tr><td>oxycodone 5mg q 6h prn or</td><td>need for IVC, minimal</td><td>SEs – constipation, drowsiness,</td></tr><tr><td>equivalent*</td><td>hypotension</td><td>delirium</td></tr><tr><td>New opioids eg targin,</td><td>Suitable for outpatient use, no</td><td>Less dependence (??) than e.g.</td></tr><tr><td>tapentadol</td><td>need for IVC, minimal</td><td>oxycodone</td></tr><tr><td>hypotension</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Parenteral opioid analgesia via</td><td>Patient-controlled, good pain</td><td>Requires IVC, SEs opiates</td></tr><tr><td>PCA</td><td>control, unlikely opioid toxicity</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Epidural</td><td>Less delirium/sedation, less resp</td><td>Requires skill for placement,</td></tr><tr><td>depression, better analgesia</td><td>hypotension, risk of</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>than PCA,</td><td>infection/haematoma, may not</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>be effective, probably CI until we</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>know his coagulopathy is</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>reversed!!! Will also limit</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>mobility- not great esp with</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>predeliction for DVT/PE</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Local/regional block eg</td><td>Possibly better analgesia than</td><td>Limited evidence for outcomes,</td></tr><tr><td>intercostal block/ serratus plane</td><td>epidural, less SEs eg</td><td>large vols LA – possibility of LA</td></tr><tr><td>block</td><td>hypotension,</td><td>tox, coagulopathic</td></tr><tr><td>Ketamine infusion</td><td>Good analgesia, little</td><td>Delirium, psychosis, limited</td></tr><tr><td>hypotension/resp depression</td><td>evidence</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td><td>FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</td><td>2020.2</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":11,"title":"","stemText":"A 24-year-old man presents to the ED saying he has been bitten by a snake","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Other than for snake bites which envenomations require a pressure immobilization bandage? (3 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"The patient is currently asymptomatic. List your priorities in assessment and initial management (4 marks) What are the indications for treatment with antivenom, both absolute and relative? (6 marks) Absolute Relative"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Following treatment he makes a full recovery and is ready for discharge. He asks your advice as he has heard of people getting sick after antivenom. How do you respond? (2 marks) Problem Time frame Symptoms Treatment"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">1</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Funnel web spider</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cone fish/snail</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Blue ringed octopus</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cameron 3rd edition pages 991+995</div><div class=\"answer-line\">A 24-year-old male presents to ED saying he has been bitten by a snake. He is currently</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">1.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2020.2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Apply PIB and immobilise patient</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. Hx and examination to ascertain likelihood of bite/envenomation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. Blood tests on arrival</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Move to appropriate area – resus</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ensure appropriate antivenom is available</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Do not release PIB unless examination and bloods are normal.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Must include application of PIB or critical fail.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cameron 3rd edition pages 985-989</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Funnel web spider</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Cone fish/snail</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Blue ringed octopus</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Cameron 3rd edition pages 991+995</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">A 24-year-old male presents to ED saying he has been bitten by a snake. He is currently</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">1.</td></tr><tr><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td><td>FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</td><td>2020.2</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">1. Apply PIB and immobilise patient</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">2. Hx and examination to ascertain likelihood of bite/envenomation</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">3. Blood tests on arrival</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Move to appropriate area – resus</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Ensure appropriate antivenom is available</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Do not release PIB unless examination and bloods are normal.</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Must include application of PIB or critical fail.</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Cameron 3rd edition pages 985-989</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">What are the indications for treatment with antivenom, both absolute and relative?</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">(6 marks)</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Absolute</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Relative</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Absolute</td></tr><tr><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td><td>FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</td><td>2020.2</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Hx of collapse/cardiac arrest/seizure</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Evidence of VICC, INR &gt;3, D Dimer &gt; 10, Unrecordable Fibrinogen</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Any neurological features</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Relative</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Significant systemic symptoms – nausea/vomiting/headache/abdo pain</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Evidence of partial VICC – INR 1-3, Low but recordable fibrinogen</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">CK &gt; 1000</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Leukocytosis</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">tools/envenomation</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Problem</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Time frame</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Symptoms</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Serum sickness from day 4-14 – rash, fever, myalgia and arthralgia</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">Prednisolone 25-50mg daily for 5-7 days but not prophylactically</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"3\">tools/envenomation</td></tr><tr><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td><td>FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</td><td>2020.2</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":12,"title":"","stemText":"A normally well 18 month old girl is brought into your ED with her parents. She has been unwell for 1 week with an URTI and fever. 3 days ago she developed a rash which started on her face and torso. The rash now involves both arms and legs and perineum. The parents have been applying wet dressings and topical steroid as per the GP for a flare of her normally mild eczema. The patient appears coryzal and miserable on first impression. An image is available in the Props Book","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Describe 3 features of the rash (3 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"What is your most likely diagnosis (1 mark)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Describe 3 investigations that you would perform in this child and explain the rationale behind each (6 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"State 6 key components of your management of the patient (6 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:imgf72f0911914466be23","asset:img4e342a896beb07fae8","asset:imgdab0324f8bff52c2bf"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">An image is available in the Props Book</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>1. monomorphic blisters/vesicles filled with clear yellow/thick purulent fluid/material </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>2. some new blisters with umbilication </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>3. some older blisters with crusting </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Some blood stained blisters (red/purple/black) </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Some secondary bacterial infection with staph or strep resulting in impetigo additionally </em></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Eczema Herpeticum (considered one of the few dermatological emergencies) </em><em>can be diagnosed </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>clinically when a patient with known atopic dermatitis presents with an acute eruption of </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>painful, monomorphic clustered vesicles associated with fever and malaise</em><em> </em></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>1. Viral swab for culture/HSV PCR taken by scraping the base of a fresh blister to confirm clinical </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>diagnosis </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>2. Bacterial swab for microscopy and culture as eczema herpeticum can be complicated by secondary </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>bacterial infection </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. Slit lamp eye exam/fluoroscein to rule out ulcer/corneal involvement</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">1. admit to hospital under Paediatrics/consult dermatology service</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2020.2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. IV acyclovir 500mg m2 dose</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. IV Flucloxacillin 50mg/kg dose 8 hourly</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4. soak to remove bacterial crusts/ topical emollient and wet dressings</div><div class=\"answer-line\">5. analgesia regular panadol and neurofen.  IN fentanyl for wet dressings</div><div class=\"answer-line\">6. Urgent ophthalmology review if eye involvement</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2020.2</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":13,"title":"","stemText":"You are a consultant in a tertiary emergency department. You are alerted via the ”bat phone” about the arrival of a 28 year old man with GCS 4 following attempted hanging. He had been found by his mother and cut down from ceiling. You have 10 minutes until his arrival. He has not been intubated.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Briefly describe the possible injury mechanisms that can occur in non-judicial hanging (5 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Outline the main steps you would undertake to prepare for this patient’s arrival? (3 marks)\nWhen he arrives he has GCS 4 with abnormal limb extension. Describe specific assessment\nfeatures in this patient with a hanging injury (8 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">You are a consultant in a tertiary emergency department. You are alerted via the ”bat</div><div class=\"answer-line\">phone” about the arrival of a 28 year old man with GCS 4 following attempted hanging. He</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">had been found by his mother and cut down from ceiling. You have 10 minutes until his</div><div class=\"answer-line\">arrival. He has not been intubated.</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":14,"title":"","stemText":"A25 year-old woman presents following a sexual assault. She had separated from her partner three months previously because of recurring verbal abuse and an escalating intravenous drug use problem. He had returned “home” last night drunk, drug affected and aggressive and had assaulted and raped her. After careful assessment it is likely that the only significant physical injury is two minor rib fractures.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Provide an estimate of the risk of HIV transmission in heterosexual sexual exposure (2 marks) Receptive vaginal intercourse Receptive anal intercourse"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"What characteristics of the assailant (assuming they are known) would increase the risk of HIV transmission to a high enough level to mandate HIV post-exposure prophylaxis? (2 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Discuss the role of presumptive antimicrobial therapy for Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonnorhoeae (3 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"The patient does not want the police involved. In what circumstances is it mandatory for a doctor to make a report of domestic and family violence to police regardless of the victim’s wishes? (4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">A25 year-old woman presents following a sexual assault. She had separated from her partner three</div><div class=\"answer-line\">months previously because of recurring verbal abuse and an escalating intravenous drug use</div><div class=\"answer-line\">problem. He had returned “home” last night drunk, drug affected and aggressive and had assaulted</div><div class=\"answer-line\">and raped her.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">After careful assessment it is likely that the only significant physical injury is two minor rib fractures.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">High prev country</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">IVDU</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Bisexual man</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Presumptive treatment is generally not indicated or recommended in current protocols.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Screening/testing for <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em>, <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (and syphilis, <em>Trichomonas ,</em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">HBV and HIV <em>is</em> recommended. Screening for Chlamydia &amp; Gonorrhoea is by swab</div><div class=\"answer-line\">(vaginal/rectal/whatever) → PCR. The identification of a sexually transmitted pathogen following</div><div class=\"answer-line\">sexual assault usually indicates a pre-existing infection. If a pathogen is identified, treat the</div><div class=\"answer-line\">infection.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">If a disclosure is made that minors witnessed this assault or were at risk there is a mandatory</div><div class=\"answer-line\">reporting requirement.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">In the absence of the involvement of minors, most jurisdictions mandate referrals to police when:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Serious injuries have been inflicted such as broken bones, stab and gunshot wounds</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2020.2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">The perpetrator has access to a gun and is threatening to cause physical injury to any person</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">The perpetrator is using or carrying a weapon (including guns, knives or any other weapon</div><div class=\"answer-line\">capable of injuring a person) in a manner likely to cause physical injury to any person or</div><div class=\"answer-line\">likely to cause a reasonable person to fear for their personal safety</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">An immediate serious risk to individual/s or public safety exists</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">An offence has occurred on NSW Health premises, or in circumstances in which health</div><div class=\"answer-line\">workers are threatened because of their professional role</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2020.2</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":15,"title":"","stemText":"There is an outbreak of a novel Ebola-like viral haemorrhagic fever in Central Africa. A Health Care Worker recently returned from volunteer work in the affected region has presented with symptoms meeting the current case definition.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Explain the meaning of “case definition” (2 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Outline the activities that EDs should undertake as part of the prevention and preparedness stage of management of pandemic illnesses (6 marks) Assuming your hospital is fully equipped for disease management; outline your management of this patient during the first 12 hours (5 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">There is an outbreak of a novel Ebola-like viral haemorrhagic fever in Central Africa. A</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Health Care Worker recently returned from volunteer work in the affected region has</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">presented with symptoms meeting the current case definition.</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":16,"title":"","stemText":"A 23 year-old female has been at a music festival for the past 2 days. Her friends have brought her to ED because she has become agitated and confused after taking recreational drugs. Her friends are unsure what drugs they were. She is usually fit and well. She is on no regular medication and has no past history of significance. On arrival she has\n• \nAxillary temperature 39.5\n• \n\nHR 120bpm\n• \n\nBP 90/60\n• \n\nGCS 11 (E3, M5, V3)","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"What are your priorities in her management? (6 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"What investigations would you perform and why? (4 marks) It becomes apparent that a number of patients were taken to local hospitals during this music festival and that there was one fatality. State government and local health officials are meeting to plan a coordinated response at future music festivals. You have been asked to attend as an emergency medicine expert. Outline the key features of this response plan that you would recommend (5 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Cooling</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Decrease heat productive - encourage inactivity in compliant patients, avoid restraints, early</div><div class=\"answer-line\">benzodiazepines, IM droperidol 10mg for agitated patients, consider early  intubation and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">paralysis for severe hyperthermia (&gt;40 degrees) with agitation and decreased level  of</div><div class=\"answer-line\">consciousness</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Aggressive active cooling - strip patient, ice packs over major vessels, spray/mist/wet</div><div class=\"answer-line\">bedsheets / fan, IVF cooled or at room temperature</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Supportive care</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Seek &amp; treat any specific toxidrome/overdose</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Seek &amp; treat complications of ingestion/hyperpyrexia – MODS, metabolic derangement etc</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Seek &amp; treat concurrent problems – trauma/assault</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Transfer to ICU - may require dialysis or ECMO</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2020.2</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">VBG for acidosis, potassium and BSL</div><div class=\"answer-line\">ECG / continuous cardiac monitoring</div><div class=\"answer-line\">EUC/CMP/CK - AKI / ARF / rhabdo /  hyperkalaemia / hypo or  hypercalcaemia  /</div><div class=\"answer-line\">hyponatraemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FBC/Coag  - DIC</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Echo</div><div class=\"answer-line\">CT brain  - if concern for trauma, not recovering as expected, focal findings</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":17,"title":"","stemText":"A 41-year-old female presented to your ED following a syncopal episode in which she fell, sustaining a minor cheek laceration. She lives alone and has no recollection of the events and says she simply found herself on the floor covered in blood. She denies medical problems although she says she has been vomiting intermittently for the last three days. She is on no regular medications. Admits to a packet of cigarettes daily and four bottles of wine a week but denies drinking in the preceding 48 hours. She has normal vital signs and a normal cardio-respiratory and abdominal examination.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"An ECG is acquired. Please describe and interpret the ECG (3 marks) An image is displayed in the Props Book"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Blood tests are displayed. List your three main concerns regarding this patient and write short notes\n• n your approach to each of these concerns (9 marks)\n\nNa 130 mmol/L (135-145)\n\nK 2.5 mmol/L (3.5-5.5)\n\nCl 93 mmol/L (97-107)\nBicarb 18 mmol/L (22-26)\n\nUrea 5.6 mmol/L (2.5-7.1)\n\nCreatinine 59 umol/L (62-106)\nAG 22 mmol/L\nBili 19 umol/L (2-20)\nAlb 44 g/L (35-50)\nALP 65 U/L (50-100)\nGGT 643 U/L (6-50)\nALT 107 U/L (5-30)\nAST 256 U/L (5-30)\nMagnesium 0.73 mmol/L (0.8-1.0)\nBHCG < 1.2 IU\n\nWCC 12.1 x 10^9 (4-10)\n\nHb 127g/L (115-160)\n\nPlatelets 105 x 10^9 (150-400)\nClinical Concern Approach"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List 4 non-ischaemic causes of T inversion or ST depression (4 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:img82985087fbe3fd52b9"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Sinus rhythm 75 bpm</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">ST segment depression inferior &amp; lateral leads</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">T wave inversion laterally</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">? QT prolonged – appears about 550mS (corrected about 625)…however could be an</div><div class=\"answer-line\">“apparent” QT prolongation which is really due to fusion of T and U waves ( = QU interval)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">? U waves – esp V4-5</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Blood tests are displayed. List your three main concerns regarding this patient and write</div><div class=\"answer-line\">short notes on your approach to each of these concerns (9 marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Na 130 mmol/L (135-145)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">K 2.5 mmol/L (3.5-5.5)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cl 93 mmol/L</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Bicarb 18 mmol/L (22-26)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Urea 5.6 mmol/L</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Creatinine 59 umol/L (62-106)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2020.2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">AG 22 mmol/L</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Bili 19 umol/L (2-20)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Alb 44 g/L (35-50)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">ALP 65 U/L (50-100)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">GGT 643 U/L (6-50)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">ALT 107 U/L (5-30)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">AST 256 U/L (5-30)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Bundle branch block</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">LVH</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Digoxin</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">CNS injury</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Myopericarditis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">PE</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hypokalaemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FE SAQ Practice Paper Sample Answers</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2020.2</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":18,"title":"","stemText":"Ambulance officers have brought a middle-aged male in cardiac arrest. The patient’s wife says he is normally well but has been complaining of chest pain and breathlessness today, prior to collapse. The patient is in pulseless electrical activity (PEA). As team leader you direct one of the team to perform a point-of-care ultrasound (POCUS) of the patient’s heart. The POCUS demonstrates organised cardiac activity. An image is available in the Props Book.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Describe and interpret this ultrasound image (3 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"What other POCUS features can be sought to help confirm the diagnosis? (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List possible underlying causes in this patient (3 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List three other causes of PEA arrest and the expected POCUS findings for each (6 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:imgf633d6e7bd1aeba03c"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Ambulance officers have brought a middle-aged male in cardiac arrest. The patient’s wife says he is</div><div class=\"answer-line\">normally well but has been complaining of chest pain and breathlessness today, prior to collapse.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">The patient is in pulseless electrical activity (PEA). As team leader you direct one of the team to</div><div class=\"answer-line\">perform a point-of-care ultrasound (POCUS) of the patient’s heart.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">The POCUS demonstrates organised cardiac activity.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">An image is available in the Props Book.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"empty-state\">The supplied marking resource did not contain extractable text for this part.</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":false,"answerSourceRetained":false}];
